{"maxId":113,"entries":{"chapter-63-study-other-neurodegenerative-disorders|Chapter 63 Study: Other Neurodegenerative Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"key-differentiators-pattern-recognition|Key differentiators (pattern recognition)":["u3"],"copy|Mixed pathology (AD + vascular + FTLD + Lewy body in one brain) is common with advancing age (p981).":["u4"],"copy|~30% of Parkinson disease (PD) patients develop dementia; unlike AD, motor symptoms predate dementia by years (p981).":["u5"],"copy|Early recurrent falls as a presenting symptom → suspect PSP, vascular dementia, or PD (p981).":["u6"],"copy|Prominent early behavior/personality change without amnesia → suspect FTD (p982).":["u7"],"copy|Vertical gaze abnormality + preserved oculocephalic reflex + falls/dysphagia → suggests PSP (p982).":["u8"],"copy|Possible bvFTD needs 3 of 6 core symptoms (early disinhibition, apathy, loss of sympathy/empathy, repetitive motor behaviors, hyperorality, frontal executive deficit with relative visuospatial sparing) (p982).":["u9"],"copy|Underlying pathology groups the syndromes: alpha-synuclein unites DLB, PDD, and MSA; tau predominates in PSP, CBD, and nfvPPA; TDP-43 in svPPA; amyloid β + tau in AD and lvPPA (Table 63-5, p993).":["u10"],"dementia-with-lewy-bodies-dlb|Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)":["u11"],"copy|Core tetrad: fluctuating cognition, spontaneous parkinsonism, RBD, recurrent visual hallucinations.":["u12"],"copy|Mean onset 75y (range 50–80y), slight male predominance (p983).":["u13"],"copy|MMSE can swing up to 50% day-to-day with fluctuation (p983).":["u14"],"copy|RBD in 85% of DLB vs 15% PD vs 60% MSA — RBD is a core diagnostic feature (p983).":["u15"],"copy|Depression in up to 40%; visual hallucinations, misperceptions or (less often) delusional misidentification in ~2/3; parkinsonism eventually in up to 70% (p983).":["u16"],"copy|Probable DLB: at least two core clinical features, or one core feature plus an indicative biomarker (DLB Consortium 2017). REM sleep behavior disorder is a core feature. For book questions, p984 discusses RBD as core, while the older Table 63-3 uses the term “suggestive” and places RBD in that category.Current practice (not the book): probable DLB = ≥2 core features, or 1 core feature + ≥1 indicative biomarker (reduced DAT uptake on SPECT/PET, abnormal MIBG scintigraphy, or PSG-confirmed REM sleep without atonia) — DLB Consortium 4th consensus report (McKeith et al.), 2017.":["u17"],"copy|MMSE decline −4 to −5 pts/yr vs ~3 pts/yr in AD — faster course than AD (p984).":["u18"],"copy|In postmortem-confirmed DLB cases, mean survival ~10y, disability progressing ~10%/yr (p983).":["u19"],"copy|One-year rule: DLB is favored when dementia starts before, concurrently with, or within one year after parkinsonism. PDD is favored when established PD precedes dementia by more than one year. The interval is an operational convention (p984, DLB Consortium 2017).Current practice (not the book): DLB is diagnosed when dementia occurs before or concurrently with parkinsonism; the 1-year rule between onset of dementia and parkinsonism is kept for research DLB-vs-PDD distinction — DLB Consortium (McKeith et al.), 2017.":["u20"],"copy|Robust cholinesterase-inhibitor response (often better than AD, especially hallucinations); often neuroleptic sensitivity.":["u21"],"parkinson-disease-dementia-pdd|Parkinson Disease Dementia (PDD)":["u22"],"copy|Distinguished from DLB primarily by timing: dementia arises years-to-decades after established motor PD.":["u23"],"copy|~1/3 of PD patients eventually develop PDD (p985).":["u24"],"copy|Possible predictors: older age at motor onset, bradykinesia/non-tremor phenotype, bilateral onset, declining levodopa response, depression, hallucinations (p985).":["u25"],"copy|DLB declines faster than PDD (p985).":["u26"],"multiple-system-atrophy-msa|Multiple System Atrophy (MSA)":["u27"],"copy|Death within 6–10y; 10-yr survival ~40% (p985).":["u28"],"copy|In older epidemiologic studies MSA incidence was about 0.6 per 100,000 person-years, and prevalence was 1.86–4.9 per 100,000 people. The chapter reports incidence rising to 6 per 100,000 person-years after age 50, mean onset at 54 years and a male predominance in its cited population (p985).":["u29"],"copy|Subtypes: MSA-P (parkinsonism) ~80%, MSA-C (cerebellar) ~20%; orthostatic-predominant subtype → worse survival (p985).":["u30"],"copy|In the 2022 MDS criteria, neurogenic orthostatic hypotension is a systolic fall ≥20 mm Hg, usually accompanied by a diastolic fall ≥10 mm Hg, with a blunted heart-rate response. The time window is 3 minutes for clinically established MSA and up to 10 minutes for clinically probable MSA. The 30-mm Hg systolic threshold on p986 reflects earlier criteria.":["u31"],"copy|Suspect an alternative diagnosis if onset <age 30 or family history present (p986).":["u32"],"copy|Sleep complaints: fragmentation 53%, early waking 33%, insomnia 20%; RBD up to 60% (p986). Stridor → worse survival/sudden-death risk (p986).":["u33"],"copy|MRI: putaminal atrophy/hypointensity; nonspecific \"hot cross bun\" sign in pons (p986).":["u34"],"copy|~1/3 respond to levodopa in early stages (p986).":["u35"],"ftld-spectrum-bvftd-ppa-variants-pick-disease|FTLD spectrum (bvFTD, PPA variants, Pick disease)":["u36"],"copy|Focal frontal/anterior-temporal atrophy; brain as light as 750 g at autopsy (p986).":["u37"],"copy|In most centers, bvFTD accounts for >50% of FTD cases; pathology ~50% tau, ~50% TDP-43, <10% FUS (p987).":["u38"],"copy|bvFTD mean onset mid-50s (p989); depression uncommon (unlike AD) (p989).":["u39"],"copy|Primary progressive aphasia: insidious, progressive language impairment is the dominant early deficit and the main initial limitation on daily activities. The book describes an older two-year isolated-language criterion (p987). The international consensus criteria do not require waiting two years to diagnose PPA. nfvPPA: apraxic/agrammatic speech, preserved single-word comprehension; left-dominant atrophy (inferior frontal/insula/caudate); ~20% TDP pathology (usually TDP-43 type A) at autopsy, most show tau (CBD > PSP > Pick) (p988). svPPA: fluent but semantically empty speech, surface dyslexia; begins in amygdala/anterior temporal lobe; most behaviorally prominent PPA; often TDP-43 type C-positive, tau-negative inclusions at autopsy (p988). Logopenic PPA = usually an AD language presentation; may respond to cholinesterase inhibitors if imaging or other biomarkers confirm AD etiology (p987-988).":["u40"],"copy|Pick disease: pathologic dx, found in ~20% of clinical bvFTD-pattern cases; Pick disease usually 3R-tau; dentate gyrus heavily involved (can be spared in AD) (p989–990).":["u41"],"copy|Genetics: MAPT mutation → FTDP-17, mean onset ~52y (p990). GRN mutation → up to 11% of sporadic / ~25% of familial FTD; ~10% of carriers remain asymptomatic past age 70; >10% show autoimmunity (p990). C9orf72 hexanucleotide expansion = the major cause of familial FTD/ALS/FTD-ALS (p990).":["u42"],"copy|Treatment: symptomatic only — SSRIs for behavioral symptoms/cravings/compulsions; atypical neuroleptics can be considered if delusions are problematic; no theoretical basis for cholinesterase inhibitors (may worsen agitation) (p991).":["u43"],"amyotrophic-lateral-sclerosis-als|Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)":["u44"],"copy|Incidence 1–2/100,000/yr; ~5,000 new cases/yr; ~2:1 M:F (p991).":["u45"],"copy|Bulbar onset in ~25% (more common in older patients); pseudobulbar affect in up to 45% (p991).":["u46"],"copy|Mixed upper + lower motor neuron signs; EMG is diagnostic.":["u47"],"copy|Only 50% survival at 3 years; aspiration/respiratory failure is the leading cause of death (p991).":["u48"],"copy|TDP-43 inclusions link ALS and FTLD: ~15% of FTLD patients develop ALS; ~50% of ALS patients develop FTLD-type cognitive/behavioral symptoms (p991).":["u49"],"corticobasal-degeneration-cbd|Corticobasal Degeneration (CBD)":["u50"],"copy|Mean onset mid-60s; asymmetric parkinsonism + apraxia + cortical sensory loss ± alien limb (p991).":["u51"],"copy|Tauopathy sharing a pathology spectrum with FTD/PSP; unlike Pick disease, ballooned neurons are extensive (primary sensory and motor regions); Pick bodies absent (p992).":["u52"],"copy|Symptomatic treatment only; levodopa helps a minority; no theoretical basis for cholinesterase inhibitors (p992).":["u53"],"progressive-supranuclear-palsy-psp|Progressive Supranuclear Palsy (PSP)":["u54"],"copy|Prevalence 1/100,000 overall, rising to 7/100,000 after age 55 (p992).":["u55"],"copy|Falls as the first symptom in 60%; downward vertical gaze palsy is more specific than upward (mild vertical gaze limitation is normal with aging), with oculocephalic reflex preserved early in disease (p992).":["u56"],"copy|Dysphagia in 46% within the first 5 years; bradykinesia in only ~25% of autopsy-confirmed cases (p992).":["u57"],"copy|Survival 6–10y from diagnosis; levodopa helps some patients but efficacy wanes (p992).":["u58"],"spinocerebellar-ataxia-sca|Spinocerebellar Ataxia (SCA)":["u59"],"copy|Heterogeneous, mostly autosomal-dominant trinucleotide-repeat expansions; numbered by discovery order; variable penetrance (p992).":["u60"],"seizures-epilepsy-in-older-adults|Seizures/epilepsy in older adults":["u61"],"copy|New cortical (lobar) hemorrhage → think cerebral amyloid angiopathy (CAA), not hypertension (hypertensive hemorrhages are typically deep) (p994).":["u62"],"copy|Metastatic tumors (lung, breast, kidney, colon/rectum, melanoma) are a more common cause of new seizures in older adults than primary brain tumors (p994).":["u63"],"copy|Epilepsy prevalence in AD: 10–22%; typically complex partial (mesial temporal) seizures — déjà vu, olfactory aura, speech arrest; can occur even with mild or pre-dementia cognitive symptoms (p994).":["u64"],"copy|Suitable AEDs in older adults (book: \"include but are not limited to\"; chosen for fewer side effects/interactions): lamotrigine, levetiracetam, lacosamide, oxcarbazepine (p994).":["u65"],"traumatic-brain-injury-tbi|Traumatic Brain Injury (TBI)":["u66"],"copy|Penetrating TBI → highest morbidity/mortality; post-traumatic epilepsy risk >50% (p994).":["u67"],"copy|Falls are the leading cause of nonpenetrating TBI in older adults; subdural hematoma is a common fall complication and can present as subacute cognitive decline (p994).":["u68"],"copy|GCS: mild 13–15, moderate 9–12, severe <9 (p994).":["u69"],"copy|Concussion/mild TBI definition: LOC <30 min, PTA <24h (p994), with an initial GCS of 13–15 (p995).":["u70"],"copy|Postconcussive syndrome occurs in 30–80%; headache, dizziness/vertigo, insomnia, irritability, impaired memory/cognition — usually resolves over months but can persist indefinitely (p995).":["u71"],"key-drugs-mentioned-no-specific-mg-doses-are-given-in-this-chapter|Key drugs mentioned (no specific mg doses are given in this chapter)":["u72"],"copy|Cholinesterase inhibitors — first-line for DLB (often better response than AD, esp. hallucinations); no theoretical basis for use in bvFTD/CBD, may worsen agitation (p984, p991–992).":["u73"],"copy|Levodopa — can be used if indicated for parkinsonism in DLB (once hallucinations/fluctuation are controlled), ~1/3 of MSA patients, and some PSP/CBD patients, but efficacy is limited/wanes (p984, p986, p992).":["u74"],"copy|Atypical neuroleptics — bvFTD delusions if problematic; use with caution/avoid in DLB (neuroleptic sensitivity) (p984, p991).":["u75"],"copy|SSRIs — bvFTD behavioral symptoms (cravings, compulsions) (p991).":["u76"],"copy|Melatonin / low-dose clonazepam — RBD in DLB/MSA (p984, p986).":["u77"],"copy|Riluzole (FDA-approved) — ALS: extends survival, delays need for ventilation (p991).":["u78"],"copy|Midodrine / fludrocortisone — MSA orthostatic hypotension (p986).":["u79"],"copy|Botulinum toxin — CBD dystonic limb, adjunct therapy (p992).":["u80"],"recall|Recall":["u81"],"copy|CHAPTER 63s / HAZZARD":["u82"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 63 — Other Neurodegenerative Disorders; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u83"],"copy|1.":["u84"],"copy|What is the \"1-year rule\" used to separate DLB from PDD?":["u85"],"copy|DLB can be diagnosed when dementia precedes parkinsonism, begins concurrently, or appears within one year of motor onset.":["u86"],"copy|PDD describes dementia after more than one year of established motor PD.":["u86"],"copy|The timing convention is described by the DLB Consortium (p984, 2017 consensus).":["u86"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): DLB is diagnosed when dementia occurs before or concurrently with parkinsonism; the 1-year rule between onset of dementia and parkinsonism is kept for research DLB-vs-PDD distinction — DLB Consortium (McKeith et al.), 2017.":["u87"],"copy|2.":["u88"],"copy|How does the typical MMSE decline rate in DLB compare to AD?":["u89"],"copy|DLB declines faster: about 4–5 points/year vs about 3 points/year in AD (p984).":["u90"],"copy|3.":["u91"],"copy|What percentage of DLB, PD, and MSA patients develop REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD)?":["u92"],"copy|85% of DLB, 15% of PD, and 60% of MSA patients (p983).":["u93"],"copy|9.":["u94"],"copy|What proportion of nfvPPA cases show TDP-43 pathology versus tau pathology at autopsy?":["u95"],"copy|About 20% show TDP-43 (usually type A); the majority show tau pathology (CBD most common, then PSP, then Pick) (p988).":["u96"],"copy|11.":["u97"],"copy|What is the approximate 3-year survival in ALS, and what is the most common cause of death?":["u98"],"copy|About 50% survival at 3 years; aspiration or respiratory failure is the most common cause of death (p991).":["u99"],"copy|13.":["u100"],"copy|In PSP, is downward or upward vertical gaze palsy more diagnostically specific, and why?":["u101"],"copy|Downward gaze palsy is more specific, because mild vertical gaze limitation is normal with aging (p992).":["u102"],"copy|Answer":["u103","u104","u105","u106","u107","u108"],"copy|Probable DLB: p984 gives two core features or one core plus one supportive feature in the prose. Table 63-3 gives two core features or one core plus at least one suggestive feature. The prose includes REM sleep behavior disorder among core features, whereas the table lists it as suggestive (p984).Current practice (not the book): probable DLB = ≥2 core features, or 1 core feature + ≥1 indicative biomarker (reduced DAT uptake on SPECT/PET, abnormal MIBG scintigraphy, or PSG-confirmed REM sleep without atonia) — DLB Consortium 4th consensus report (McKeith et al.), 2017.":["u17"],"copy|One-year rule, as printed: p984 says the research criteria require cognitive symptoms that “predate parkinsonism by 12 months.” The chapter calls the temporal distinction arbitrary and difficult to apply (pp984–985). Ch 57, p870, describes DLB when dementia precedes or occurs within one year of motor onset, and PDD when dementia develops after a year of established motor PD.Current practice (not the book): DLB is diagnosed when dementia occurs before or concurrently with parkinsonism; the 1-year rule between onset of dementia and parkinsonism is kept for research DLB-vs-PDD distinction — DLB Consortium (McKeith et al.), 2017.":["u20"],"copy|Ch 63, p984, prints “predate parkinsonism by 12 months” as the research criterion.":["u112"],"copy|This conflicts with the timing described in Ch 57, p870.":["u113"],"copy|There, DLB includes dementia preceding or occurring within one year of parkinsonism, and PDD describes dementia after a year of established motor PD.":["u86"],"copy|CHAPTER 63 / HAZZARD":["u82"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[114,-5],[117,0]]}
